  <main class="site-page network-page" id="main-content" tabindex="-1">
    <header class="page-header network-header">
      <a class="page-back" href={`${base}/`}>&larr; The Zone</a>
      <h1 class="page-title">Network</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="network-lede" data-network-lede data-lede-variant="marginal">
      <div class="lede-copy">
        <p class="prose-copy">In typical Pynchon fashion, paranoia pervades <i>Gravity's Rainbow</i>: <span class="lede-term-pair" data-lede-anchor="puritan"><a href={`${base}/glossary/#glossary-puritan-paranoia`} data-lede-term-copy>Slothrop's Puritan reflex</a><button class="lede-term" type="button" data-lede-trigger data-quote="a Puritan reflex of seeking other orders behind the visible, also known as paranoia" data-episode="2.01" aria-controls="network-lede-tooltip" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Slothrop's Puritan reflex</button></span> seems to infect everything that he touches, turning pure coincidences into conspiratorial structures. Nothing unusual, though, since <span class="lede-term-pair" data-lede-anchor="conditioning"><span data-lede-term-copy>Pavlovian psychology</span><button class="lede-term" type="button" data-lede-trigger data-quote="No effect without cause, and a clear train of linkages." data-episode="1.13" aria-controls="network-lede-tooltip" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Pavlovian psychology</button></span> has long ago taught us that associations are the backbones of human understanding and memory. Such is the way that the novel expands around associations, conditioning, drawing seemingly far-fetched (<i>who knows?</i>) connections between our little paranoia agents, who all oscillate between the <span class="lede-term-pair" data-lede-anchor="anti-paranoia"><span data-lede-term-copy>fear that nothing relates</span><button class="lede-term" type="button" data-lede-trigger data-quote="anti-paranoia, where nothing is connected to anything" data-episode="3.12" aria-controls="network-lede-tooltip" aria-expanded="false" hidden>fear that nothing relates</button></span> and the <span class="lede-term-pair" data-lede-anchor="everything"><span data-lede-term-copy>suspicion that everything does</span><button class="lede-term" type="button" data-lede-trigger data-quote="the discovery that everything is connected, everything in the Creation" data-episode="4.07" aria-controls="network-lede-tooltip" aria-expanded="false" hidden>suspicion that everything does</button></span>. So, what better way to analyse <i>Gravity's Rainbow</i> than looking at these networks between characters, names and words?</p>
        <p class="prose-copy">This, of course, has been done countless times before: <a href="https://thomaspynchon.com/rocket-power-gravitys-rainbow-reviewed-by-richard-poirier-1973/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Poirier</a> already traced these networks in his 1973 review; <a href="https://doi.org/10.1177/096394700301200102" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Luc Herman, Robert Hogenraad and Wim van Mierlo</a> (2003) measured chapter-level word covariation; <a href="https://eve.gd/2015/06/07/visualizing-gravitys-rainbow/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Martin Paul Eve</a> (2015) mapped frequent terms and the connections between them; <a href="https://doi.org/10.16995/orbit.131" target="_blank" rel="noopener">David Letzler</a> (2016) combined social-network analysis with word frequency, keyness and topic modelling. <a href="https://krmuth.github.io/plot-gr/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Katie Muth</a> has also made interactive three-dimensional networks of the novel. On this page, I provide my own attempt to visualise its various webs.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="lede-notes" aria-label="Quoted passages from Gravity's Rainbow" data-lede-notes>
        <blockquote class="lede-note" data-lede-note="puritan">
          <p>‘a Puritan reflex of seeking other orders behind the visible’ <span class="lede-note-episode">(2.01)</span></p>
        </blockquote>
        <blockquote class="lede-note" data-lede-note="conditioning">
          <p>‘No effect without cause, and a clear train of linkages.’ <span class="lede-note-episode">(1.13)</span></p>
        </blockquote>
        <blockquote class="lede-note" data-lede-note="anti-paranoia">
          <p>‘anti-paranoia, where nothing is connected to anything’ <span class="lede-note-episode">(3.12)</span></p>
        </blockquote>
        <blockquote class="lede-note" data-lede-note="everything">
          <p>‘the discovery that everything is connected’ <span class="lede-note-episode">(4.07)</span></p>
        </blockquote>
      </aside>
