    <section class="network-section matrix-section">
      <div class="matrix-stage">
        <h2 class="matrix-heading" id="cooccurrence-title">Co-occurrence</h2>
        <figure class="section-chart" aria-labelledby="cooccurrence-title">
          <NetworkMatrix />
        </figure>
        <div class="section-prose matrix-copy">
          <p class="prose-copy">'They are in love. Fuck the war.' (1.06). The lovebirds Roger and Jessica share only eleven episodes compared to Katje and Slothrop's sixteen, the latter obviously leading by count; then why do we have <a href="https://themillions.com/2016/03/gravitys-rainbow-a-love-story.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">so much more attachment</a> to the former's relationship? In this matrix we don't simply count co-occurrence episodes: it's much less surprising to find two characters present together in a crowded episode, compared to when a pair of characters dominate <i>their own</i> episodes. The resulting measure is 'prominence-corrected co-occurrence': <button class="matrix-pair-ref" type="button" data-matrix-pair="Jessica Swanlake|Roger Mexico" data-matrix-colour="var(--network-counterforce)" aria-controls="matrix-plate" aria-label="Highlight Roger and Jessica's co-occurrence" aria-pressed="false">Roger and Jessica</button> reach 1.6, the highest weight in the matrix, whereas <button class="matrix-pair-ref matrix-pair-ref--zone" type="button" data-matrix-pair="Katje Borgesius|Tyrone Slothrop" data-matrix-colour="var(--network-quest)" aria-controls="matrix-plate" aria-label="Highlight Katje and Slothrop's co-occurrence" aria-pressed="false">Katje and Slothrop</button> have only 0.9. Amongst the forty names we see four out of the five communities identified in the Character atlas above. <button class="matrix-pair-ref" type="button" data-matrix-pair="Jessica Swanlake|Roger Mexico" data-matrix-colour="var(--network-counterforce)" aria-controls="matrix-plate" aria-label="Highlight Roger and Jessica's co-occurrence" aria-pressed="false">Roger and Jessica</button> form London's strongest tie, <button class="matrix-pair-ref matrix-pair-ref--rocket" type="button" data-matrix-pair="Enzian|Tchitcherine" data-matrix-colour="var(--network-rocket)" aria-controls="matrix-plate" aria-label="Highlight Enzian and Tchitcherine's co-occurrence" aria-pressed="false">Enzian and Tchitcherine</button> Rocket's; <button class="matrix-pair-ref matrix-pair-ref--cross-community" type="button" data-matrix-pairs="Ned Pointsman|Tyrone Slothrop;Tchitcherine|Tyrone Slothrop;Marvy|Tyrone Slothrop;Tyrone Slothrop|Weissmann / Blicero;Enzian|Tyrone Slothrop;Laszlo Jamf|Tyrone Slothrop;Roger Mexico|Tyrone Slothrop" data-matrix-colour="var(--color-text-dim)" aria-controls="matrix-plate" aria-label="Highlight Slothrop's seven strongest cross-community co-occurrences" aria-pressed="false">Slothrop</button> appears in seven of the ten strongest cross-community pairs. This result concurs with prior analysis by <a href="https://doi.org/10.16995/orbit.131" target="_blank" rel="noopener">David Letzler</a> (2016), which identifies Slothrop with Tantivy and Roger with Jessica as strongest. <a href="https://eve.gd/2015/06/07/visualizing-gravitys-rainbow/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Martin Paul Eve</a> (2015) groups Roger, Jessica, and Pointsman, using frequent-term proximity across the whole text rather than episode co-occurrence. The methods are <a href={`${base}/methods/#matrix`}>here</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="network-section episode-section" aria-labelledby="mention-density-title">
      <div class="episode-stage">
        <figure class="section-chart episode-chart" aria-labelledby="mention-density-title">
          <CharacterEpisodeHeatmap>
            <h2 slot="title" class="episode-heading" id="mention-density-title">Character-mention heatmap</h2>
            <div slot="reading" class="episode-reading">
              <p class="prose-copy">The twenty characters on this heatmap have the highest raw mention totals across the novel. Strictly speaking, the plot has each character's mentions in an episode <i>per</i> 1,000 words, normalised within-character so that the final value is within the 0-to-1 range, with that character's own maximum set to 1; finally, the characters are ordered, from top to bottom, by their peak-mention episodes. Switch to <a href="#episode-heatmap" data-density-mode-toggle aria-label="Switch to raw density">raw</a> density here to compare <i>across</i> characters. Raw counts, episode lengths and the calculation are in the <a href={`${base}/methods/#character-trajectories`}>Methods</a>.</p>
              <p class="prose-copy">Here we see <button class="matrix-pair-ref episode-ref" type="button" data-trajectory-select="6,8" aria-controls="episode-heatmap" aria-label="Highlight Roger and Jessica's mention trajectories" aria-pressed="false">Roger and Jessica</button> flare together briefly at episode 1.07 and <span class="episode-possessive"><button class="matrix-pair-ref matrix-pair-ref--zone episode-ref" type="button" data-trajectory-select="4" aria-controls="episode-heatmap" aria-pressed="false">Slothrop</button>'s</span> burst of activity at the start of Part II as we move to continental Europe; we see the brothers <button class="matrix-pair-ref matrix-pair-ref--rocket episode-ref" type="button" data-trajectory-select="16" aria-controls="episode-heatmap" aria-label="Highlight Tchitcherine's mention trajectory" aria-pressed="false">Tchitcherine</button> and <button class="matrix-pair-ref matrix-pair-ref--rocket episode-ref" type="button" data-trajectory-select="11" aria-controls="episode-heatmap" aria-label="Highlight Enzian's mention trajectory" aria-pressed="false">Enzian</button>, then <button class="matrix-pair-ref matrix-pair-ref--pokler episode-ref" type="button" data-trajectory-select="17,9,18" aria-controls="episode-heatmap" aria-label="Highlight Pökler, Jamf and Bland's mention trajectories" aria-pressed="false">Pökler, Jamf and Bland</button> near the end of Part III.</p>
              <p class="prose-copy">I loved working through this analysis, as seeing each character cluster brought back so many scenes and memories.</p>
            </div>
          </CharacterEpisodeHeatmap>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="network-section cast-section" aria-labelledby="cast-composition-title">
      <div class="cast-stage">
        <h2 class="cast-heading" id="cast-composition-title">Cast transitions</h2>
        <div class="section-prose cast-copy cast-copy--intro">
            <p class="prose-copy"><i>Gravity's Rainbow</i> is characterised, to a great extent, by how much the cast rotates through the episodes. One moment we are in Slothrop territory, and the next we are recounting Pökler's engineering years. Such transitions are here visualised with stems whose heights measure how sharply the relative weight of character sets change between episodes: a low stem means the current episode keeps a similar cast balance as the last, whereas a high stem marks a break in narrative attention.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="section-prose cast-copy cast-copy--detail">
            <p class="prose-copy">At <button class="matrix-pair-ref cast-ref" type="button" data-cast-boundary-select="39" aria-controls="cast-composition-canvas" aria-label="Inspect 3.10 to 3.11 (C)" aria-pressed="false">C</button> we can see the aforementioned Slothrop-to-Pökler shift, a digression episode not dissimilar to the famed Webb Traverse episode early in <i>Against the Day</i>, bringing about some of the most emotionally charged writing in Pynchon's catalogue. <button class="matrix-pair-ref cast-ref" type="button" data-cast-boundary-select="14" aria-controls="cast-composition-canvas" aria-label="Inspect 1.14 to 1.15 (A)" aria-pressed="false">A</button> and <button class="matrix-pair-ref cast-ref" type="button" data-cast-boundary-select="15" aria-controls="cast-composition-canvas" aria-label="Inspect 1.15 to 1.16 (B)" aria-pressed="false">B</button> move from Blicero's fairy-tale battery to Darlene and then Roger and Jessica; <button class="matrix-pair-ref cast-ref" type="button" data-cast-boundary-select="53" aria-controls="cast-composition-canvas" aria-label="Inspect 3.24 to 3.25 (D)" aria-pressed="false">D</button> is a digression to the theology of William Slothrop... And so on. The calculation is in <a href={`${base}/methods/#cast-composition`}>Methods</a>.</p>
        </div>
